WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:01.489
Speaker 1: All right.

00:00:01.489 --> 00:00:03.870
I have something amazing for you guys.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:11.620
Within just a couple of weeks of the leaked
news that the Supreme Court will soon be overturning

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:19.580
Roe v Wade, 2022 House of Representatives
polling has completely reversed, and in a

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:28.810
new NPR PBS Marist National poll, Democrats
are now plus five over Republicans looking

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.440
at the November congressional elections.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.250
Now, we're not going to go crazy and get ahead
of ourselves.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.630
I'm not going to start ripping apart my microphone
and burning it and celebrating anything.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:39.740
Not that I would do that.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.820
Anyway, we're going to look through this piece
by piece.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:48.420
And in order to do that, we have to first
start with what would we expect in such a

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:49.420
midterm?

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:57.990
Typically in a midterm, the party that is
not in the White House does well right now.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:58.990
Democrats are in the White House.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.390
Joe Biden took the White House from Trump
in 2020.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:07.180
And so we would expect Republicans to do really
well in the midterms.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.570
Now, let's look at some previous examples.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:17.229
In 2018, after Donald Trump took power in
2016, Democrats were plus seven in polling

00:01:17.229 --> 00:01:22.030
going into the midterms, and they ended up
plus eight in an aggregate of congressional

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:23.030
races.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.729
Okay, Trump wins next midterm.

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:27.189
Democrats +78.

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:28.859
In the 2014 midterms.

00:01:28.859 --> 00:01:33.799
After Barack Obama won reelection, Republicans
were plus two, and they ended up winning by

00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:35.679
almost plus six.

00:01:35.679 --> 00:01:37.299
Obama reelection.

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:39.950
Republicans win 2010.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:45.270
Barack Obama had just won in 2008 over John
McCain taking the White House from Republicans

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.340
previously held by George W Bush.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.630
Obama wins 2008 2010.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:56.939
Republicans were plus nine in the midterms,
ended up plus seven in those midterms.

00:01:56.939 --> 00:01:58.450
Disastrous midterms for Democrats.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:01:59.450
Okay.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:05.139
So normally these first midterms, in particular,
the first ones.

00:02:05.139 --> 00:02:09.260
But even after a Democratic reelection, you
expect to see Republicans do well.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:12.700
We now have a stunning new poll.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.080
These numbers, you really have to see them
to believe them.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:23.500
47% of registered voters now say they will
support the Democrat on the ballot in this

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:29.270
year's congressional midterm, while 42% say
they will back the Republican.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:35.290
That is plus five for Democrats in advance
of these midterms.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.050
Now, we want to be thorough.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.890
We don't look at just one poll.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:41.980
We look at an average of recent polls.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:47.410
And if you look at the Real Clear Politics
average of recent polls, you still have Republicans

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:51.780
plus two looking at the generic congressional
vote.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:58.630
Now, that is dramatically lower than it has
been in any recent first midterm after the

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:02.670
party controlling the White House changes
power and it is declining.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:11.660
Whereas Republicans were polling about an
average of 48% in February and about 47% in

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:17.470
mid-March, and they peaked at 47% again in
mid-May.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.970
Now Republicans are down from where they were.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.490
Could Republicans still will win?

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:24.490
Of course.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:25.490
Of course.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:26.490
It's still the most likely scenario.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:31.870
This is just one poll, but we are now seeing
multiple polls in an economist YouGov poll.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:37.470
Democrats are leading a congressional vote
generic in a Politico morning consult poll.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.470
Democrats are leading.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:46.200
And yes, in that recent NPR PBS Marist poll
I just mentioned, Democrats are now plus five.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:54.030
What this means is that the midterm might
not be over the impact of the Roe v Wade news,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.120
especially if.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:00.880
And when it becomes official, when the Supreme
Court finally announces its ruling, it could

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.880
really give Democrats a boost.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.490
And what it means at the individual level
is every one of us must vote.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:10.230
If you've ever voted before, you should vote.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:13.320
If you've ever thought about voting, you should
vote.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.110
If you've never voted, you should vote.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:19.430
If you weren't old enough to vote in 2020,
but now you're old enough to vote legally,

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.469
of course you should vote.

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:25.419
If you've ever heard of voting, like if that's
a concept you've ever heard about, you should

00:04:25.419 --> 00:04:31.240
vote because it doesn't have to be the disaster
that it was shaping up to be.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:38.479
It seems as though the forthcoming Roe v Wade
decision is absolutely hurting Republicans.

00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:43.909
One other interesting note from that poll,
which I think just should be mentioned, is

00:04:43.909 --> 00:04:46.729
on the question of overturning Roe v Wade.

00:04:46.729 --> 00:04:52.560
64% of Americans do not believe Roe v Wade
should be overturned, basically by a 2 to

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.370
1 margin, two thirds, one third.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.919
Americans do not want the Supreme Court to
overturn Roe v Wade.

00:04:58.919 --> 00:05:04.500
So if and when that happens, it could be exactly
the boost on which Democrats might be able

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:05.509
to ride to.

00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:07.529
Could it be victory in November?

00:05:07.529 --> 00:05:08.529
Maybe.

00:05:08.529 --> 00:05:13.270
But could it be a much, much smaller loss
than what it was looking like three months

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:14.319
ago?

00:05:14.319 --> 00:05:15.510
That very much could be.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:21.490
And as I've said before, Joe Biden hasn't
done that much to help Democrats in November.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:25.150
And we'll take all the help we can get to
get these Republicans or keep these wacky

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:30.430
Republicans out of power if they want to hurt
themselves by virtue of their own Supreme

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:35.389
Court picks, making a very unpopular decision
when it comes to Roe v Wade.

00:05:35.389 --> 00:05:36.860
I'll take the help from anybody.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:38.479
We can get it.

00:05:38.479 --> 00:05:39.479
Anybody we can get it from.

